# The working tree must equal the committed bytes on every platform.
#
# Two byte-exact seals depend on it and both were broken by its absence:
# `release/dependency-locks.sha256` hashes Cargo.lock and pnpm-lock.yaml, and
# `MANIFEST.sha256` hashes every delivered source file. GitHub's Windows runners
# set core.autocrlf=true, so a checkout there rewrote every text file to CRLF and
# the freeze recorded hashes of a working tree that exists only on Windows --
# measured in P60 run 34746478525: the artifact's Cargo.lock hash is exactly
# sha256(committed bytes with LF replaced by CRLF).
#
# `-text` rather than `text=auto eol=lf`: no conversion in EITHER direction, so
# the working tree is the committed bytes and nothing is renormalized. eol=lf
# would have rewritten 37 files that are committed with CRLF -- captured Windows
# console output under docs/phase36/evidence/ and one .ps1 -- and the CRLF in
# captured evidence is part of what was captured. It also leaves scripts/*.cmd
# exactly as committed on every platform instead of picking a side.
#
# It does not affect diffs: EOL conversion is the `text` attribute, binary
# detection for diff is content-based and separate.
* -text
